Silt-Graft

Ingredients: GOTHIC ROCK & DEATHROCK + K-R&B + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Rhythm Acoustic Guitar
Creative Catalyst: The genre honors a lost pirate radio signal that only intercepted airwaves during coastal thunderstorms.

Silt-Graft is an unsettling, highly melismatic sonic collision where the aggressive, angular dread of 1980s deathrock meets the silk-smooth vocal ornamentations and deep 808 sub-bass of modern Seoul R&B. Driven by frantic, dry-recorded rhythm acoustic guitars picked with furious post-punk velocity, the tracks swim in lush, tape-saturating reverbs and cold subterranean frequencies. Melancholy Korean vocal lines slide breathlessly over jagged, flange-soaked basslines, creating an atmosphere that feels simultaneously like a luxury late-night listening lounge and an abandoned wet-timber mausoleum.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 78–92 BPM. Double-time acoustic strumming (borrowed from deathrock's nervous urgency) sits atop a heavy, half-time R&B swing with deep sub-bass dropouts.
- Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Phrygian dominant modes, utilizing minor 9th chords, sudden diminished shifts, and unresolved suspended intervals.
- Production Signatures: Tape-flanged acoustic guitar tracking, spatialized vocal "spectral tail" delays that vanish into simulated atmospheric static, and low-pass filtered 808 kicks sidechained directly to brittle drum-machine hi-hats.
- Dynamics: Extreme micro-dynamics; lingering, whispered vocal isolations abruptly collapse into violent, wall-of-sound acoustic strumming and low-end thrums.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the mid-2010s within the flood-prone subterranean music hideouts of Incheon’s industrial port district, Silt-Graft emerged from a tiny collective of art-school dropouts and late-night broadcast enthusiasts. The scene grew around "Station 94.7-Silt," a phantom pirate radio frequency that only managed to overpower local emergency channels during heavy downpours. Dressed in oversized black silk overcoats, silver-pierced velvet collars, and salt-stained combat boots, partygoers would gather in dripping concrete storm cellars, sipping cold barley tea spiked with dark plum liquor. They danced with slow, fluid R&B body-rolls interrupted by sudden, sharp deathrock contortions, honoring the ghostly transmission that died forever when the local harbor tower was demolished in 2019.

Instruments Used
- Rhythm Acoustic Guitar: Nylon and steel-string acoustic guitars played with rapid down-strums, processed through chorus pedals and tape delay.
- Vocal Processing & Synths: Analog pitch-shifting processors, KORG MS-20 for sub-bass, and vintage Roland VP-330 Vocoders for choir-like ghostly undertones.
- Rhythm Section: Roland TR-808 kick sub-bass layered with dry, organic shaker patterns and gated post-punk snare hits.

For Fans Of: Dean, Christian Death, Part Time Punks, SAAY, Sex Gang Children

